 {"acceptance_criteria":"1) A capability probe reports the Landlock ABI (and what it can enforce) per host, alongside the existing pidns-capability-probe.py and fs-isolation-probe.py. 2) When the host permits Landlock but denies uid mapping, the jail enforces a filesystem tier (at minimum: deny reads of the agent credential surfaces — ~/.ssh, ~/.config/terminal-jail token files, hermes secret env, other users' homes — and deny writes outside the working tree) instead of degrading silently to 'no filesystem isolation'. 3) Where Landlock cannot enforce, behaviour is unchanged and still warns loudly; the tier is reported per host in --help/README (docs row DOC-8 tracks the capability matrix). 4) Tests cover: enforcement active on a permitting host (prove a denied read returns EACCES), graceful absence on a non-permitting host, and no regression to the existing unshare/bwrap paths. 5) The per-host report is machine-readable so the fleet can record which tier each image actually gets. 6) OWNERSHIP (decided 2026-10-01 by live experiment): this tier belongs to terminal-jail, NOT bunker -- Landlock enforces on a plain host with no userns and no root (the exact deployment case this tool exists for: dev box, CI runner, agent shell without a container), and it also enforces inside this tool's own jails, so it composes. Moving it to the platform would leave every non-bunker deployment on the DEGRADED tier. When bunker hosts the agent, its container boundary is the OUTER layer and Landlock is defence in depth, not duplication.","attempts":0,"created_at":"2026-09-27T10:53:48-05:00","created_by":"carter review 2026-09-27 (hands-on, commit 6f614783)","decision":"OWNERSHIP DECISION 2026-10-01 (Bane: 'are these right for terminal-jail or should they be in bunker?'; decided by live experiment, not opinion): the tool owns per-command JUDGEMENT and every guarantee it can hold on ANY host without root; the platform (bunker) owns ambient NAMESPACE/LIFECYCLE primitives. Experiment, this host (Ubuntu 26.04, kernel 7.0.0-31, apparmor_restrict_unprivileged_userns=1): (a) private /proc via unshare is UNREACHABLE here -- `unshare --user --map-root-user --mount --mount-proc` rc=1 'write failed /proc/self/uid_map: Operation not permitted', and without --map-root-user rc=1 'cannot change root filesystem propagation: Permission denied'; bwrap is the only in-tool route and is an optional system package; (b) Landlock enforces with NO user namespace and NO root: as plain uid 1000 (ABI 8) a DAC-readable file became 'DENIED (Permission denied)' after restrict_self, and /etc/hostname was denied too; (c) Landlock also enforces INSIDE both of this tool's jails (uid 65534, identical results) -- it composes with the wrapper instead of fighting it. LAYERS, NOT EITHER/OR (Bane 2026-10-01): each is a standalone building block -- sometimes only terminal-jail is wanted, sometimes only bunker -- and the strongest configuration is the pair, tool inside the platform. The composed stack is not automatic today: measured, the tool REFUSES to run inside a container (`namespace creation failed (unshare exit 1)`) while the container already provides a private /proc and its own PID ns. See the composed-mode row filed from the same experiment.","description":"Measured 2026-09-27 on Ubuntu 26.04 / kernel 7.0.0-31: the jail's filesystem half is ABSENT by default. `terminal-jail --user sh -c ...` prints 'no filesystem isolation — could not create a uid mapping under the user namespace (this host denies setuid inside unprivileged user namespaces, e.g. Ubuntu AppArmor profile 'unprivileged_userns')' and scripts/fs-isolation-probe.py classifies the host DEGRADED (mapped launch rc=1, 'setgroups failed: Operation not permitted'). Only the PID/identity layers hold, so a jailed process still reads the caller's files. This is the same wall Anthropic's sandbox-runtime documents for Ubuntu 24.04+ (kernel.apparmor_restrict_unprivileged_userns strips capabilities from the namespace), so it is an ecosystem fact — but it is the largest single reduction of the project's containment promise on the distro most users install. Landlock needs no uid mapping and no capability-bearing user namespace: landlock_create_ruleset(VERSION) returns ABI 8 on this kernel, and ABI >= 1 already gives filesystem access control. Precedent: OpenAI's Codex CLI uses Landlock for its Linux sandbox. This also subsumes the report's 'srt-style allow/deny path policy' suggestion — a Landlock ruleset IS a kernel-enforced path policy, which is strictly stronger than enumerating secret-read paths in regexes.","detail":"Source: hands-on review 2026-09-27, report /home/kara/terminal-jail-carter-review-2026-09-27.html at commit 6f614783.","id":"TJ-GAP-082","owner":"terminal-jail","priority":"P2","status":"complete","title":"[P2] Filesystem isolation has no working tier on Ubuntu AppArmor hosts: add Landlock (ABI v8 here) as a uid-mapping-free filesystem tier so containment does not depend on setuid-in-userns","type":"task","worker_status":"committed","commit_hash":"bd39236089763d3b82ce30a70c2834cf457c7979","guard_result":"tier1 PASS 3/3 (lint/secrets/tests); uvx ruff check . = All checks passed","ci_result":"pushed bd39236; post-push CI run pending at board time","completed_at":"2026-10-07T01:31:14-05:00","foreman_note":"Landlock FS-isolation tier landed (worker glm-5.3-flash@zai-glm-default, ~5.5h session). Probe: scripts/landlock-capability-probe.py -> FULL, ABI 8, enforcement EACCES-proven (property preflight per TJ-DF-015). Suite 1574+6skip -> 1604+7skip (+31 landlock tests), foreman re-run green on HEAD. Tier2 judge INCOMPLETE-by-402 (deepseek-v4-flash provider payment error, verdict b10cf732) — disclosed infra skip; compensating evidence: tier1 PASS 3/3 + foreman full-suite re-run + live probe outputs quoted in ch:trace footer of bd39236.","judge_verdict":"b10cf732 tier2 INCOMPLETE (402 provider outage), tier1 PASS"}
-{"acceptance_criteria": "1) With the egress tier enabled, a payload that opens a TCP connection to a non-allowlisted host/port is denied by the KERNEL even when no regex matches it — demonstrated with a case that defeats the current patterns (e.g. a raw python socket or a protocol tunnel). 2) An allowlist exists for legitimate traffic (git push over ssh/https, package registries) and is documented; the deny is fail-closed, the allow is explicit. 3) Verdict/reason for a Landlock denial is distinguishable from a rule-engine denial, so an operator can tell 'a rule said no' from 'the kernel said no'. 4) Degrades honestly on hosts without Landlock ABI >= 4: no silent no-op — either the tier reports unavailable or the request to enable it fails closed. 5) The existing regex class keeps its tests; the new tier's tests prove kernel enforcement, not pattern matching.", "attempts": 0, "created_at": "2026-09-27T10:53:48-05:00", "created_by": "carter review 2026-09-27 (hands-on, commit 6f614783)", "description": "TJ-GAP-058 closed the network-egress rule CLASS with regexes (/dev/tcp, nc -e, socat EXEC, curl -T, wget --post-file) and it is complete; the review's 22-payload matrix confirms three of those block with quotable rule ids. But regex matching has to out-guess every client, and the review's own honest-gaps list still names 'network egress class' as the largest hole. Landlock ABI >= 4 adds per-port TCP bind/connect control enforced by the kernel, which turns the allowlist from a pattern problem into a policy problem: deny connect except an enumerated port set, regardless of which binary or encoding is used. ABI 8 is available on this kernel. This complements, never replaces, the regex class (regex catches intent and gives a human-readable reason; Landlock catches the syscall).", "detail": "Source: hands-on review 2026-09-27, report /home/kara/terminal-jail-carter-review-2026-09-27.html at commit 6f614783.", "id": "TJ-GAP-083", "priority": "P2", "status": "pending", "title": "[P2] Network egress is still evaluable only by regex: add Landlock TCP port rules (ABI >= 4) as a kernel-side egress allowlist behind the rule class", "type": "task"}
+{"acceptance_criteria": "1) With the egress tier enabled, a payload that opens a TCP connection to a non-allowlisted host/port is denied by the KERNEL even when no regex matches it — demonstrated with a case that defeats the current patterns (e.g. a raw python socket or a protocol tunnel). 2) An allowlist exists for legitimate traffic (git push over ssh/https, package registries) and is documented; the deny is fail-closed, the allow is explicit. 3) Verdict/reason for a Landlock denial is distinguishable from a rule-engine denial, so an operator can tell 'a rule said no' from 'the kernel said no'. 4) Degrades honestly on hosts without Landlock ABI >= 4: no silent no-op — either the tier reports unavailable or the request to enable it fails closed. 5) The existing regex class keeps its tests; the new tier's tests prove kernel enforcement, not pattern matching.", "attempts": 0, "created_at": "2026-09-27T10:53:48-05:00", "created_by": "carter review 2026-09-27 (hands-on, commit 6f614783)", "description": "TJ-GAP-058 closed the network-egress rule CLASS with regexes (/dev/tcp, nc -e, socat EXEC, curl -T, wget --post-file) and it is complete; the review's 22-payload matrix confirms three of those block with quotable rule ids. But regex matching has to out-guess every client, and the review's own honest-gaps list still names 'network egress class' as the largest hole. Landlock ABI >= 4 adds per-port TCP bind/connect control enforced by the kernel, which turns the allowlist from a pattern problem into a policy problem: deny connect except an enumerated port set, regardless of which binary or encoding is used. ABI 8 is available on this kernel. This complements, never replaces, the regex class (regex catches intent and gives a human-readable reason; Landlock catches the syscall).", "detail": "Source: hands-on review 2026-09-27, report /home/kara/terminal-jail-carter-review-2026-09-27.html at commit 6f614783.", "id": "TJ-GAP-083", "priority": "P2", "status": "complete", "tit