 {"acceptance_criteria": "1) A resident mode exists (documented, optional, no new hard dependency) serving the SAME verdict contract as the stdin bridge; a client that cannot reach it falls back to the one-shot path rather than failing open silently. 2) Measured before/after on the same host with the same payloads: bridge round-trip p50 reported, target well under 10 ms, methodology stated (sample size + host). 3) The daemon is not a new attack surface: no TCP listener, socket permissions documented, and the 'no network surface' property (currently 0 grep hits for any listener) is preserved. 4) A regression guard pins the one-shot path's cost so it cannot silently degrade (see TJ-GAP-086). 5) Docs state which integration the reader should choose and why, with the measured numbers.", "attempts": 0, "created_at": "2026-09-27T10:53:48-05:00", "created_by": "carter review 2026-09-27 (hands-on, commit 6f614783)", "description": "Measured 2026-09-27 on the fresh clone: in-process intercept() is p50 2.19-2.24 ms (n=200) and sustains 360 verdicts/s, but the consumer path a host actually pays — the JSON bridge as a subprocess — is p50 65.5 ms / mean 71.2 ms / max 137.6 ms (n=50), dominated by interpreter startup. The firewall is therefore effectively free as a library and ~30x more expensive as the documented integration surface, which matters for a host that fires one verdict per tool call. Fix: a small resident process over a Unix socket in front of the same engine, keeping the one-shot stdin path as the fallback.", "detail": "Source: hands-on review 2026-09-27, report /home/kara/terminal-jail-carter-review-2026-09-27.html at commit 6f614783.", "id": "TJ-GAP-084", "priority": "P3", "status": "pending", "title": "[P3] Per-command cost is 65 ms because of Python startup, not judgement: add an optional resident bridge and a documented per-command budget", "type": "task"}
 {"acceptance_criteria": "1) The verdict (and/or a documented debug surface) states which rule file/source decided, its priority, and whether it overrode a builtin of the same id. 2) A same-id override that lowers a builtin (e.g. block -> warn) is reported at decision time, not silently applied: the reason string names the overriding file. 3) A CLI surface replays a decision in one line for a given command (the bridge can already do this — surface it as a verb/flag so an operator does not have to pipe JSON by hand; cf. Codex's `sandbox` debug). 4) Tests pin the shadowing case: a stale/duplicate builtin copy in the user dir must be visible in the verdict, and a downgrade must be reported. 5) Documentation states the resolution order and how to detect a shadowing copy in one command.", "attempts": 0, "ci_result": "15/15 recent runs success (run 36760493704 on 626f7e8)", "commit_hash": "93ecf3b", "completed_at": "2026-10-01T04:32:13+00:00", "created_at": "2026-09-27T10:53:48-05:00", "created_by": "carter review 2026-09-27 (hands-on, commit 6f614783)", "description": "The engine returns a quotable rule_id and reason — the strongest property of this firewall — but not the identity of the layer that won: engine builtin vs shipped YAML mirror vs user rule dir vs rule pack. That gap already cost this project a wave: TJ-GAP-051 (complete) records a shipped YAML mirror being loaded as USER rules and weakening 12 engine verdicts, and the same shadowing class was re-hit during the TJ-GAP-053 wave with a stale installed copy at ~/.config/terminal-jail/rules.d/00-builtins.yaml. Today the user-visible remedy is a documented same-id override that can LOWER a rule to warn — a legitimate feature — with nothing in the verdict or any log recording that a builtin was downgraded, so an operator cannot see the downgrade without reading the rules tree by hand. The review's observability lens flagged this as the one tamper-adjacent gap in an otherwise attributable design.", "detail": "Source: hands-on review 2026-09-27, report /home/kara/terminal-jail-carter-review-2026-09-27.html at commit 6f614783.", "foreman_note": "Verification-only close: layer provenance (engine/yaml/user/pack) fully landed at 93ecf3b via wt/TJ-GAP-085 merge 1b70a24; prior FAIL verdicts were tier1-tests-lane timeouts (Command timed out) with tier2 criterion FAIL only pre-merge. Foreman re-ran full suite at HEAD: 1558 passed / 7 skipped (156s); fresh tier2 judge at HEAD (95aca1e1): COMPLETE; live bridge probes layer=yaml/engine correct.", "guard_result": "Tier1 DEGRADED PASS (lint/secrets clean; tests lane timeout host-load class); foreman standalone full suite 1558 passed / 7 skipped at HEAD", "id": "TJ-GAP-085", "priority": "P2", "status": "complete", "title": "[P2] A verdict names the rule id but not which FILE decided it: expose rule resolution provenance so a shadowing/override cannot hide (TJ-GAP-051 class)", "type": "task", "updated_at": "2026-10-01T04:32:13+00:00", "worker_status": "complete", "worker_summary": "Layer provenance landed pre-tick: InterceptResult.layer (engine/yaml/user/pack), Decider maps exactly the dirs the RuleLoader walked (last-loaded source wins, same-id override semantics); bridge serializes layer on engine verdicts and null on both error envelopes; 12 new pins incl. shipped-mirror-as-user-dir (layer=yaml) and downgraded-builtin-names-the-user-layer."}
 {"acceptance_criteria": "1) A stated policy: either an enforced/documented maximum argument size with an explicit, clear refusal above it, or a documented linear-cost budget with the measured curve. 2) A committed benchmark (small, bounded, runnable on a laptop) that reports p50/p99 for a small, an 8 KB, and a 20 KB argument, plus a ceiling assertion so a regression beyond the pinned numbers fails. 3) The policy is visible where an integrator reads it (README/bridge docs), not only in a test. 4) Regression coverage for the TJ-DF-024 class stays green under the new guard.", "attempts": 0, "created_at": "2026-09-27T10:53:48-05:00", "created_by": "carter review 2026-09-27 (hands-on, commit 6f614783)", "description": "Measured 2026-09-27 on the fresh clone (n=200 each): p50 21.8 ms for an 8 KB argument and 51.7 ms for a 20 KB argument, against 2.2 ms for ordinary commands — cost grows linearly with argument size, so a host that pipes whole files through the firewall pays seconds per call. TJ-DF-024 (complete) fixed the acute case (one 8 KB argument cost ~7.5 s of CPU inside intercept(); now 22 ms), but nothing states a policy or pins the behaviour: there is no documented maximum, no guard against the size that makes a host stall, and no benchmark in CI. The report's framing: fine for a shell command, a real ceiling for file-sized input.", "detail": "Source: hands-on review 2026-09-27, report /home/kara/terminal-jail-carter-review-2026-09-27.html at commit 6f614783.", "id": "TJ-GAP-086", "priority": "P3", "status": "pending", "title": "[P3] Argument-size ceiling is unbounded and linear: 8 KB = 22 ms, 20 KB = 52 ms, no documented policy or guard", "type": "task"}
